meta { title = Your first program, order = 2, group = Learn }

A runnable project exports a function named main. The CLI and playground call that function and show its returned value.

h2 { Names and results }

Use = to bind a value to a name. Arithmetic uses familiar operators such as + and *. A function returns its final expression; you do not need a return keyword.

Indent the body beneath fn. These lessons use two spaces. Source files use the .kalandra extension.

h2 { Try it }

This program calculates six times seven, names the result answer, and returns it. Change either number and run it again.

{{example:first-program}}

h2 { Run it locally }

Install the CLI using the installation guide, then save the example above as main.kalandra in a new directory.

codeblock { language = bash, "kalandra run main.kalandra --strict" }

The result is 42. You can also use `kalandra new my-app` to start a project with two modules and a parallel function.

link { url = /docs/getting-started, Installation and project setup }

h2 { When something is wrong }

Compiler errors prevent execution. In the playground, the diagnostic includes its code and a source location. Warnings may still allow a run. A failure while the program is running appears in the output panel.

